# Start the services first (Bff.Services, port 5210), then this BFF (port 5116).
# The mobile application keeps its token itself and sends it in the Authorization header.
@mobile = http://localhost:5116
@alice = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
@bob = 22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222

### Login: the application receives its token (for real: OpenID Connect + PKCE, then the phone's secure storage)
# @name login
POST {{mobile}}/mobile/v1/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "user": "alice", "password": "demo" }

### Home v1: balances and 3 latest operations, shortened labels, a few hundred bytes
GET {{mobile}}/mobile/v1/home
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.token}}

### Home v2 (new version of the application): in addition, an offer banner. The v1 is still served.
GET {{mobile}}/mobile/v2/home
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.token}}

### Operations in pages of 10: follow the "next" field (opaque cursor)
# @name page1
GET {{mobile}}/mobile/v1/accounts/{{alice}}/operations
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.token}}

###
GET {{mobile}}/mobile/v1/accounts/{{alice}}/operations?cursor={{page1.response.body.next}}
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.token}}

### Transfer
POST {{mobile}}/mobile/v1/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "source": "{{alice}}", "target": "{{bob}}", "amount": 10 }

### Without a token: 401
GET {{mobile}}/mobile/v1/home
